 Linux Processes – Beginner Notes
🔹 What is a Process?
A process is simply a program that is currently running on a system.

Example:
Running ls → creates a process
Running a Python script → creates a process

 Program = file
Process = program in execution

🔹 Process ID (PID)

Each process is assigned a unique number called a PID (Process ID).

Used to identify and manage processes
PIDs increment as new processes are created
🔹 Viewing Processes
1. View current user processes:
ps
2. View all processes (system + users):
ps aux
3. Real-time monitoring:
top
Shows CPU usage, memory usage, and active processes
Updates dynamically

💡 ps = snapshot
💡 top = live monitoring

🔹 Managing Processes
Kill a process:
kill <PID>
Common Signals:
SIGTERM → Graceful termination (recommended)
SIGKILL → Force kill (no cleanup)
SIGSTOP → Pause process
🔹 Process Hierarchy
The system starts with a parent process (like systemd)
All other processes are child processes
systemd
 ├── ssh
 ├── apache
 └── user commands

💡 Every process has a parent-child relationship

🔹 Starting Services (systemctl)

Command format:

systemctl [option] [service]

Examples:

systemctl start apache2
systemctl stop apache2
systemctl enable apache2
systemctl disable apache2
start → run service now
stop → stop service
enable → start at boot
disable → prevent auto-start
🔹 Foreground vs Background Processes
Foreground:

Runs in terminal and shows output

echo "Hello"
Background:

Runs without blocking terminal

echo "Hello" &
Send process to background:
Ctrl + Z
Bring back to foreground:
fg
🔹 Key Takeaways
Process = running program
PID = unique identifier
Use ps, ps aux, top to monitor
Use kill to terminate
Use systemctl to manage services
Use &, fg, Ctrl+Z for process control
